Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:10:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/11] TCSocket: no SIGPIPE on send, retry on EINTR

Send() used send(..., 0). When the peer has closed the connection,
the next send raises SIGPIPE, and the default action ends the whole
process, so one dropped link stopped every module. Use MSG_NOSIGNAL so
the error comes back as EPIPE and the normal close and reconnect path
runs.

Also retry a send that a signal interrupts (EINTR) instead of treating
it as a closed link, and do not call send() on a closed socket (fd -1).
---
 TCSocket.cpp | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/TCSocket.cpp b/TCSocket.cpp
index 0defd81..1dab87f 100644
--- a/TCSocket.cpp
+++ b/TCSocket.cpp
@@ -130,10 +130,17 @@ bool CTCSocket::Send(const STCPacket *packet)
 			return true;
 		}
 	}
+	if (m_Pfd[pos].fd < 0)
+		return true; // not connected; the caller reconnects
+
 	unsigned count = 0;
 	auto data = (const unsigned char *)packet;
 	do {
-		auto n = send(m_Pfd[pos].fd, data+count, sizeof(STCPacket)-count, 0);
+		// MSG_NOSIGNAL: a peer that closed must give EPIPE, not a SIGPIPE
+		// that ends the whole process.
+		auto n = send(m_Pfd[pos].fd, data+count, sizeof(STCPacket)-count, MSG_NOSIGNAL);
+		if (n < 0 && EINTR == errno)
+			continue;
 		if (n <= 0)
 		{
 			if (0 == n)
